WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.575
What if AI inference could become a thousand times faster, using physics

00:00:04.575 --> 00:00:05.933
from the nineteen eighties?

00:00:05.933 --> 00:00:06.951
That's the post.

00:00:06.951 --> 00:00:07.970
Here's the paper.

00:00:10.415 --> 00:00:11.520
Hi, I'm Merrow!

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.938
CRAM takes an energy from nineteen eighty-five, learns its costs from solved

00:00:15.938 --> 00:00:18.148
puzzles, and reasons by rolling downhill.

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:21.244
Clamp the clues of a Sudoku.

00:00:21.244 --> 00:00:23.038
Fill every other cell at random.

00:00:23.038 --> 00:00:24.832
Now walk downhill on an energy.

00:00:25.212 --> 00:00:29.296
When any chain's energy falls below a threshold fitted from the training

00:00:29.296 --> 00:00:30.657
solutions, the search stops.

00:00:32.575 --> 00:00:34.056
Here is the energy, hand-set.

00:00:34.056 --> 00:00:37.610
Every pair of cells in the same row, column or box that

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:39.683
holds the same digit costs one unit.

00:00:40.103 --> 00:00:43.543
Move one cell at a time to its cheapest digit, and the

00:00:43.543 --> 00:00:45.836
board parks in a valley with clashes left.

00:00:45.836 --> 00:00:46.982
No single change helps.

00:00:47.403 --> 00:00:48.323
So shake it.

00:00:48.323 --> 00:00:51.694
Pick a clashing cell, redraw its digit with a little temperature.

00:00:51.694 --> 00:00:53.227
Sometimes the energy goes up.

00:00:53.227 --> 00:00:54.760
Then it finds the floor.

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:58.871
A valid board has energy zero, so the search knows when it is done.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:01.917
Training learns the costs.

00:01:01.917 --> 00:01:04.351
Pull down the energy of each solved board.

00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:07.057
Push up the boards one cell away.

00:01:07.057 --> 00:01:10.380
No search is unrolled, so nothing is backpropagated through time.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:14.165
Same energy, different objectives.

00:01:14.165 --> 00:01:18.041
Pseudolikelihood reaches ninety-nine percent in forty-eight milliseconds.

00:01:18.421 --> 00:01:21.371
Backprop through one step takes four point four seconds.

00:01:21.371 --> 00:01:23.666
Full backprop through time never gets there.

00:01:25.217 --> 00:01:27.060
The learned weights are the rules.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.745
Positive costs on exactly the eight hundred and ten pairs of cells

00:01:30.745 --> 00:01:32.895
that share a row, column or box.

00:01:32.895 --> 00:01:35.045
My own laptop fit found the same.

00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:39.898
These are training times, on one H two hundred.

00:01:39.898 --> 00:01:44.073
Sudoku trains about a hundred thousand times faster than the quickest baseline.

00:01:44.473 --> 00:01:46.047
Maze, fifty-five thousand.

00:01:47.073 --> 00:01:50.614
The thousand comes from one row: N-Queens, against HRM.

00:01:51.014 --> 00:01:52.616
And CRAM is timed warm.

00:01:52.616 --> 00:01:56.139
Charge it the start-up the baselines pay, and that row lands

00:01:56.139 --> 00:01:57.420
right around a thousand.

00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:00.878
The post promised faster inference.

00:02:02.407 --> 00:02:07.344
The paper measures training time, and inference in counted operations, not seconds.

00:02:07.344 --> 00:02:08.989
No special hardware anywhere.

00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:12.541
Then the authors set the weights by hand.

00:02:12.541 --> 00:02:14.986
One number solves every Sudoku in the test set.

00:02:15.366 --> 00:02:16.690
Three solve every maze.

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:20.992
A perfect score on these benchmarks shows a few pairwise rules, nothing more.

00:02:22.558 --> 00:02:25.413
The speed is real, and the benchmarks are too easy.

00:02:25.413 --> 00:02:26.840
CRAM proves both at once.

00:02:29.608 --> 00:02:33.404
An old energy, learned in milliseconds, and a warning about Sudoku.

00:02:33.404 --> 00:02:34.439
Thanks for watching!

00:02:34.859 --> 00:02:36.878
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:36.878 --> 00:02:37.455
I'm Merrow.

00:02:37.455 --> 00:02:37.743
Bye!
